 of those XML  elements in the symbolic XML so this is  actually telling you which rules are  which elements these different selectors  are targeting I'm only showing the first  three rules here of the style sheet so  then how do we actually use this  information if we have an XML document  sort of behind this CSS style sheet and  one of the things that we can get from  this is the inheritance so inheritance  doesn't make sense if all you have is  the style sheet but if you have the  document then you can start to think  about it so in this case these two rules  from that style sheet we pulled out in  the last slide you'll see that the h1  element is a descendant of the body  element just because we know and well  from language the position is more  deeply nested there and the thing that  we notice is that if you're familiar  with CSS the color property is an  inheritable property in CSS so if we  were to just run the Cascade we would  actually miss the color property and so  we have another function CSS inheritance  that you give the position of the  element that you're interested in and it  additionally runs the inheritance  algorithm to pull any inherited  properties into the list of Wolfram  language options that it resolves now I  went through a lot of rigmarole to show  you how to sort of play with some of  this data by hand but if you're sort of  lazy you can  import directly to a stylesheet the  downside to this is that there well  there's no restriction on the style name  that you can use and so what I've done  is I've just taken the the selector and  that's what I've used to name by default  the different styles so in this case  I've just chosen an easy one h1 but if I  made this like h1 descendant h2 then you  get some pretty ridiculous style names  that might not look so fun in the  Wolfram language but you can still use  them or if you just want to understand  or analyze the CSS data itself you can  do the raw data import and you'll just  have no interpretations this is actually  the fastest import because the  interpretations are actually the  bottleneck of this importer and even  though I don't have a CSS exporter to go  along with this this would be the  easiest stuff to re-export back out to  CSS format so what I hope to do is  continue this project so this is what  I've been doing in my vast spare time is  a front-end developer my co-workers  chuckle at that so one of the  motivations I actually had for this is I  wanted to make an SVG importer and then  I realized since SVG is just an  implementation of XML it needs to be  compliant with XML it needs to  understand CSS so if I actually want an  SVG importer at some future date then we  should probably have a CSS importer that  understands all of CSS and the reason I  wanted to make this open source is this  is way too big for me like the web is  expanding very quickly in its technology  and like I've only done the core and a  few of the level 3 modules and as it  keeps going maybe other developers could  work on including other properties and  if you want to do all this it's actually  fairly documented so I made sure to  write fairly good documentation and so  within the package itself there is quite  a much larger tutorial trying to show  the comparison between  the Wolfram language and CSS and in the  future github page there's also already  instructions on how to add to this  project once it becomes available to the  public and to respect their ZnO speaker  after this talk so should I continue or  do you want questions if you have any  because I have two bonus material slides  I'll try and get through them okay so so  one of the cool things that we can do in  the Wolfram language now is start a web  session so what I've gone ahead and done  is started a Chrome session using the  start web session function then we can  do things like navigate to the Wolfram  home page and then do fun things like  grab all the P elements the paragraph  elements of that page and then if you  know JavaScript you can do some things  to pull out the property and value pairs  of the page of those paragraph elements  that we pulled out and then you'll  notice that there's actually 280  properties that we just pulled from each  of these paragraphs so each paragraph  has 280 computed properties but we only  understand 71 of them so there's room to  grow here but we get some of the more  core ones like fonts colors and margins  and so the kind of cool thing is you  could basically programmatically  navigate a web page and then say hey I  like that item on that page I want to  see its properties and then through a  little bit of JavaScript you could just  directly import use the Cascade function  and so pull things like font color which  is this nice gray color so I'm pretty  sure I pulled out this first paragraph  here but anyway so that's one sort of  workflow that I foresee in the future  the other workflow I foresee is working  with symbolic XML so this is more  getting your hands dirty and to convert  directly to a wolfram stylesheet so I  just have a helper function here to  create  I'll data cells and this is using the  same XML data that I had imported before  and so what I can do is make a body cell  style and then I can pull out some other  styles like h3 h1 list items etc I have  a special case here which is a an  element an XML element that's very  similar in style to another one except  for just the color change so I just  complement those two together and then  long story short we've got a nice style  sheet with all of those styles that we  wanted to have I can save that style  sheet and then here my grand finale  whatever so what I have on the left and  what I have on the right is the Wolfram  language notebook and on the left is the  content and styling of this basic web  page I made so I don't think that's too  bad it's got the background the text  yeah so hopefully in the future this  will get even better once you know more  features get added but thanks for your  time and I'll take any questions  [Applause]
 

